WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.100
Hi so I'm super
excited to be here.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.870
So when I was starting
college, I was super excited

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.410
because I was going to
be doing math and science

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.130
and I would just get to work
with equations and numbers

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.260
and do experiments and
play with liquid nitrogen.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.810
And I would never have to
write anything ever again.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.610
And I managed to
make it through,

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:33.890
I would say a lot of
my undergraduate career

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.950
without that being
too incorrect,

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.600
which might say something
about my scientific education.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.700
But when I got to
graduate school,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.250
I quickly realized that you
couldn't just do the science.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.330
You actually also
had to talk about it.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.570
And an excellent talk here
in this room yesterday

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.010
by Lewis Kirvan.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.650
Sometimes you just need words.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.950
So I was super fortunate
to have some mentors who

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.810
helped me learn how to write
and communicate, tell a narrative.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:10.560
So I turned the corner and
I'm OK with writing now.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.400
I got a lot more practice at it.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.150
So much so now that
if you work with me,

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.390
one of the things as a part
of onboarding to work with me

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.290
is that Hadley will tell
someone that my love language is

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:25.640
Google Docs.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.860
So now as Open Source
Program Director at RStudio.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.020
Now Posit.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.920
If you work with me, expect
to see a lot of Google Docs.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.350
Great to have just
met you Elliot.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.490
Expect some Google Docs.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.300
So I'm pretty
squared away on that.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.220
But you know, there's
still presentations

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.730
and I do like talking about
ideas, so I'll submit the talk.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.460
I'm super excited about it.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.060
I'll even write the talk.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.650
And then when it comes to doing
the slides, I am never excited.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.680
I'm never thinking like, oh,
this is going to be great.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.660
I'm going to sit
down to do my slides.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.690
I'll be able to put stuff
right where I want it

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.190
on the slide, my data and code.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:09.470
I'll just be able to integrate
it seamlessly into my slides

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.960
and if I make any
changes, no big deal,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.710
it'll just automatically update.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:18.760
That has not once been a thought
that has gone through my mind.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.500
Until now.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.060
Until Quarto.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.850
So Quarto is making
presentations fun again.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:30.640
Or for me, at least
for the first time.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.970
I am so excited about Quarto
that I would sing about it,

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.020
but that is not an area
of strength for me.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.910
So instead I have the
words to favorite things

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:46.750
and I'm going to revise
these words for Quarto.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:52.870
So you may be familiar with
this component of the song.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.510
Again, not an area of strength,
but it is kind of a good tune.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.910
So like I might accidentally
sing. So raindrops on roses

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:04.780
and whiskers on kittens, bright
copper kettles and warm woolen

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.010
mittens, brown paper
packages tied up with string.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.570
These are a few of
my favorite things.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:15.850
I do like most of those things,
but I do also like Quarto.

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:19.870
So raindrops on roses
instead of raindrops on roses

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:23.650
I am going to talk
about visual edits.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.810
So I mentioned one
of the things that I

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.960
found frustrating is that
I couldn't figure out

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.310
where I'm going to put
stuff on my slides.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.960
So this is a very
straightforward slide list item

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.760
one, two, three.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.740
But what I want
to show you is how

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.610
I did this when I was
creating this slide.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:46.060
So I have my code here.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.325
Is this video going to play?

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.130
There we go.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.210
So I can switch to visual.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.110
I can see it.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.720
Here is source or as visual.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.120
I can add in my list item here.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.610
Switch back to source.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.940
There it is as source.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.830
And then if I render
it, it's going

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.230
to render right here in preview.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:13.800
So I can really integrate
where I'm writing my code

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.209
and being able to see
things right away.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:18.360
So that's a really
big deal for me

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.960
because that's how I
put my slides together.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:23.510
All right.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.180
Whiskers on kittens.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:29.830
Kittens are one of my favorites,
but one of my other favorites

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.930
is columns and tables.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.060
So again, when you're
thinking about how

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.800
to construct your
slides, how you lay

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.140
things out is really important.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.970
So if I'm thinking,
oh, what are some

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.270
of my other favorite things
besides Quarto, ice cream's,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.470
top of the list, R and Python.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.370
I do like both.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.330
I'm so excited about Posit.
Least favorite things.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.410
Banana flavor.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:54.920
Fine as a banana, not a good
flavor in anything else.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.060
I know people have different
opinions - slamming doors,

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.000
nails on chalkboards, locking
keys in the car to refer

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.150
to Julia and Max's.

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:02.990
I have done that.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.880
I like still fear it actually.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.850
So what if I wanted to
add another column, put

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.760
in an image, no big deal.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.530
And when I say no
big deal, let's

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.270
take a look at the code that
goes along with columns.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.010
Right this is pretty
straightforward.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.710
We have hey, we're going
to do some columns.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:25.970
And then we have the columns and
the information that's in them.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.090
We can also change the
column width super easily.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.300
So there's a lot of flexibility
in how we put things together.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.660
And again, combined
with that visual editor,

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:37.210
let's try some stuff
out, see how it looks.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.460
I would almost say,
like I've never

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.580
had sort of this much power
of placement in my slides

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.032
unless it was like HTML tables.

00:05:45.032 --> 00:05:46.990
But I know we're not
supposed to compare things

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:51.630
to HTML, so forget I said that.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:52.750
So tables.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.930
Tables are another
thing that has never

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.730
been a joyful experience in
my presentation creation.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.000
So this is a table.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.680
I took some data
from scraped reviews

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.950
of ice cream from a
few different websites

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:11.580
and which are the most
favorite ice cream flavors.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.740
So I took this data and
I put it into a table.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.090
This is just a kable
table, so really

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.340
not a lot of complexity
to this table,

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.720
but integrates straight
in with my slides.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.440
I can do a gt table.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.020
So this is a little
cooler looking, right?

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.140
It has a little bit of
color, better formatting.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:36.960
These are really some of the
fundamental things around gt.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.240
I'm not using all the
fancy features here,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.690
but everything that's
here is possible for you.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.830
So what I really love
about any system.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.340
When we talk about
being code first

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.020
and people are
talking about the UI

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.370
is what this makes possible.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.580
So like where you can
start and then where

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.470
you can go with
things is like only

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.470
limited by your imagination
and how much time you

00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.830
want to spend formatting.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.600
So great.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.160
My slides are doing great.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.740
Now I'm going to have
to give my presentation.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.170
So bright copper kettles.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.230
I like kettles.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.080
Fine, I guess I like tea.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.100
I had to look up what a copper
kettle was actually, but notes

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:19.930
for the speaker.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.930
So that's another thing that
you want as a presenter.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:26.680
So I did mention I don't love
the presentation component,

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:28.180
so when I'm putting
together a talk,

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.760
I actually start with the notes.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.890
And so it's really
nice to be able to have

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.550
those notes there for me.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.160
And then build the
slides actually

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.860
kind of in the reverse order.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.640
So here's just a regular sign,
a slide with Speaker notes,

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.370
slide content.

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:50.050
This is the code that
would generate that slide.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:53.500
And then this is what it
looks like to me as a speaker.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.170
So I have the slide
that I'm talking about,

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:58.520
then I have the time.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.900
And then I have in that bottom
right where the speaker notes

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:05.110
go, and then my next
slide, everything that you

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.200
would hope for as a speaker.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.300
And again, I just
really appreciate

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.360
how it's all integrated
into one document.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.790
So you're really
working just like start

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:19.930
to finish in one place and
not having to context which

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.690
has been a really nice feature.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.620
Oc and next we have
warm woolen mittens.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:29.890
I like normal mittens.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:34.890
My hands are always cold, so
code first keeps things stable.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.270
It's a bit of a stretch.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.960
Had to rhyme.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:41.750
But this is really
about what I referred to

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:45.780
at the beginning around the
idea of reproducibility.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.860
So as Garrick mentioned
in my introduction,

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:51.710
I spent many years
teaching people

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.710
about how to work with
data, and code in a research

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:57.140
context, good practices.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.870
And so the
reproducibility component

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:05.000
is essential when we're thinking
about scientific communication.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.800
And so within Quarto, the
slides, the figure and the code,

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.370
they're all integrated.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:16.040
And so I've had situations where
I put together a presentation

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.970
and I had a figure
in that presentation.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.910
I wanted to use
that figure again,

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:21.650
but I couldn't
actually remember how

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:24.080
I had generated that figure.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.040
We don't have that
problem here, right?

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.660
It's all integrated together.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.890
And so I think about sharing
things with collaborators,

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.680
but I also especially
think about sharing things

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.310
with future me because
I don't necessarily

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.820
remember what past me did
and past me doesn't always

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.770
make good choices.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:45.350
And so the more I can
document, the more things

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:50.000
are integrated, the easier
it makes to reproduce things,

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.960
to share, to communicate
effectively and accurately

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:57.650
what we're doing, especially
in a research context.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:02.480
So we can see here, again, this
is just straightforward ggplot

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:04.130
code.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:08.120
And then here we have the graph
that this code generates here

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:10.370
below just all right, together.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:14.840
So in general, right, I wouldn't
show the code necessarily along

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.060
with the figure,
but now if you go

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:19.310
get the slides you
have right there,

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.170
the code that went into
the figure, there's

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.240
no questions about
what it was that I

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.070
was communicating in this talk.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.350
And you probably
really want to know

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:29.960
the relationship between
the average star rating

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.790
and numbers of reviews
in ice cream flavors.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:36.310
I wouldn't say there's really
any positive particular trends

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:36.810
here.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:41.490
I wouldn't do a
statistical analysis.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.680
Ok next we come to
Brown paper packages.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:50.460
And this is very apt with themes
to delight in OK so themes

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:56.130
to delight in again kind of
a standard, boring slide,

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.330
but we can easily
change our themes.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:01.230
So I used the Super
exciting default theme

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:03.660
in this presentation.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.230
Aesthetics are something
I'm working on as well,

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:11.740
and so you can see
that's going to work.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:13.240
Yes.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.420
But I can change the theme.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:21.000
So Quarto has some built in
themes, dark being one of them.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.520
And so you see I re-render
the theme changes.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:25.540
I'm ready to go.

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:29.280
I'm going to try solarized,
which I actually really like.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.140
That's another theme.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.420
So these are the
built-in themes.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.810
But again, if we refer back
to one of the talks yesterday

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:38.580
that said something
you really can

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:42.180
do to help yourself a
lot is to build themes

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.820
for your organization, for
yourself that you use time

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.460
and again that match
whatever it is that you need

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.770
to be doing for
your organization,

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:52.950
for yourself, meet whatever
standards you need.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.290
And so you could spend a lot of
time building your own theme.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:58.080
But I bet already
people in this room

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:00.720
are starting to
make amazing themes.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.630
And so the opportunity to
share these with each other

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:06.450
and easily switch back and
forth is really meaningful

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.910
because I'm not
spending time recreating

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:10.480
my slides every time.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:15.600
I can just change the theme and
have a different look and feel.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:16.300
All right.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:21.030
Finally, we're tied up with
string, and formats to share.

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:23.840
So tied up with string
is really the end.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:25.800
Right you have
your presentation,

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.410
you need to share it.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.930
Mine and Julie did a
great job this morning

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:34.780
talking about all the different
formats that you can share in.

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:37.900
So this here is an
HTML presentation.

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:38.650
That's great.

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:41.040
It works right in my browser.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.040
I didn't have any
issues with that.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:47.130
And it also is really
good for accessibility

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:48.930
because you can make
use of all the things

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:54.030
that are already built into HTML
around screen readers, colors,

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.730
so people are able to
make use of the settings

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:58.780
that they have in
their browser already.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:01.830
When you're sharing
HTML, lots of times we do

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:07.470
need PDFs to share, distribute,
print out and carry around

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:08.910
with us.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:12.920
So PDFs are something that is
straightforward from these as

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:13.420
well.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:15.390
Again, all the same
document creating

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.400
all of these different formats.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.190
And finally, I really
appreciate the work

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:23.970
that the Quarto team has put
into making PowerPoints.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:27.960
There are a lot of organizations
that need to use PowerPoints,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.540
so if you are in one
of those organizations,

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:32.700
you are probably
not culturally going

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:35.460
to be able to shift your
entire organization over

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:39.480
to using Quarto, one because
culture change is hard.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.950
But two, there are requirements
in government agencies and drug

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:47.550
discovery around the kinds
of information that you share

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:49.000
and how you share it.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.170
So I think what we've talked
about a lot in this meeting is

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:55.080
meeting people where they are,
understanding what people need

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:57.660
and building towards that
rather than saying like,

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:01.050
oh, you need to be doing
this or some idea of what's

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:04.920
best is really creating
opportunities for people

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.043
to do what it is they need
to do in their context.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:14.130
So PowerPoint is one you
would change in that header

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:15.558
where we are changing the theme.

00:14:15.558 --> 00:14:17.850
You can at the beginning say
that you want a PowerPoint

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:21.160
output and you can make a
PowerPoint output from within.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.730
Quarto and again, you
have all those advantages

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.360
of being able to integrate your
code and your visualization

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:31.500
and you're not doing what I
did would be like, get it out

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:35.310
of R, put it into Illustrator,
then take it from Illustrator

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:36.760
and put it into PowerPoint.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.720
So those things are at least
a little bit behind me.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:43.680
But looking ahead, I'm so
excited about the opportunities

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:45.990
that this presents.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.120
So here we go.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.510
This is how we've
rewritten the Quarto song

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.520
favorite things for Quarto.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:56.020
So it does rhyme.

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:56.530
All right.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:59.460
So visual edits,
columns and tables,

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.770
notes for the
speaker codes first

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:05.860
keep slide stable in student
Leyden and formats to share.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:08.243
These are a few of
my favorite things.

00:15:15.475 --> 00:15:17.350
I couldn't not sing it
because I've literally

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:21.040
had it in my head for like
two weeks straight.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:25.360
So these are some places
where you can learn more.

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.380
There's a lot of
documentation on the Quarto website.

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:30.430
You've been sent there for
a lot of different reasons.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:31.700
Quarto presentations.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:33.170
This slide deck is in GitHub.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:34.990
If you want kind of
the basics of how

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:39.070
to get started on things,
you should subscribe

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:40.780
to the glimpse newsletter.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.523
We had the first one come out
just a couple of weeks ago.

00:15:43.523 --> 00:15:45.190
We'll have another
one in August that'll

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:48.550
have lots about our tools
and how to use them,

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:51.040
especially after this meeting.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.260
So I'm just excited to be here.

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:56.390
For me, it was just such a
joy to make this presentation.

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:58.600
I so appreciate
the work the Quarto team

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.230
has done, how they've
listened to people like me

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:05.000
and thinking about how they need
to communicate their results.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:06.910
And I'm just so
excited to see what

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:09.340
you do with Quarto,
what you share,

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.700
and what you communicate
about to make the changes that

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:16.250
are important in your work and
your life and your communities.

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:18.240
Thank you so much.

